WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
This grid is the Spaceflight Fleet.

00:00:02.603 --> 00:00:16.587
One hundred thirty-seven hardware entries — every rocket, every crewed spacecraft, every rover, every space station, every observatory, every cargo vehicle ever flown or seriously planned.

00:00:16.587 --> 00:00:21.272
The catalogue is organized into nine categories along the left.

00:00:21.272 --> 00:00:22.016
Launchers.

00:00:22.016 --> 00:00:23.355
Crewed spacecraft.

00:00:23.355 --> 00:00:24.619
Cargo spacecraft.

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:25.289
Stations.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:25.810
Rovers.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:26.405
Landers.

00:00:26.405 --> 00:00:27.074
Orbiters.

00:00:27.074 --> 00:00:28.115
Observatories.

00:00:28.115 --> 00:00:29.380
Concept vehicles.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.206
Click any category to filter the grid.

00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:33.842
Try crewed spacecraft.

00:00:33.842 --> 00:00:40.834
You'll see Vostok at the top — Yuri Gagarin's vehicle, the first time a human flew into space.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:41.801
Then Voskhod.

00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:42.396
Mercury.

00:00:42.396 --> 00:00:42.917
Gemini.

00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:43.735
Apollo CSM.

00:00:43.735 --> 00:00:47.974
Soyuz, in its many generations — Seven K, T, TM, TMA, MS.

00:00:47.974 --> 00:00:53.404
Then Shuttle Orbiter, Shenzhou, Crew Dragon, Starliner, Orion, Gaganyaan.

00:00:53.404 --> 00:00:58.611
Six decades of human spaceflight engineering, lined up across one row.

00:00:58.611 --> 00:01:02.478
Each Soyuz generation distinguishable from the last.

00:01:02.478 --> 00:01:06.644
Click any card to read what changed between generations.

00:01:06.644 --> 00:01:07.759
Click Saturn V.

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:16.536
The right panel opens with the spacecraft's anatomy — every stage, every engine, drawn as a hand-authored SVG diagram.

00:01:16.536 --> 00:01:25.982
The flights tab lists thirteen Apollo missions Saturn V launched, with every crew name, every payload, every mission's outcome.

00:01:25.982 --> 00:01:33.866
The links tab points to engineering reports, oral histories, and the Marshall Space Flight Center archive.

00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:35.651
Try filtering by status.

00:01:35.651 --> 00:01:36.172
Failed.

00:01:36.172 --> 00:01:37.288
You'll see N-1.

00:01:37.288 --> 00:01:38.255
Schiaparelli.

00:01:38.255 --> 00:01:38.999
Beresheet.

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:39.668
Hakuto-R.

00:01:39.668 --> 00:01:41.602
Vikram from Chandrayaan-2.

00:01:41.602 --> 00:01:42.122
Mars 2.

00:01:42.122 --> 00:01:42.643
Hitomi.

00:01:42.643 --> 00:01:50.230
Failed missions are paired in this catalogue with their successors — the spacecraft that made it work.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.759
Try the epoch timeline at the top.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:02:02.279
Drag the time chips to filter by era — Sputnik era, Mercury-Gemini-Vostok era, Apollo era, Shuttle era, ISS era, commercial era.

00:02:02.279 --> 00:02:07.783
Compare what existed in 1965 to what existed in 1995 to what exists today.

00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:09.866
Notice the cross-references.

00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:15.072
When you click a launcher, the panel lists every mission it ever flew.

00:02:15.072 --> 00:02:22.585
When you click a mission on the Missions screen, the panel links back to the launcher that put it up.

00:02:22.585 --> 00:02:30.320
Hardware and missions reference each other in both directions — bidirectionally validated at build time.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.775
One hundred thirty-seven entries.

00:02:32.775 --> 00:02:33.667
Sixty years.

00:02:33.667 --> 00:02:34.708
Many builders.

00:02:34.708 --> 00:02:35.824
Read the names.

00:02:35.824 --> 00:02:37.163
Look at the dates.

00:02:37.163 --> 00:02:38.279
See the ladder.

